WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.384
CSP is built from directives,

2
00:00:03.464 --> 00:00:09.695
and each directive controls a category of
resource or behavior.

3
00:00:09.775 --> 00:00:17.967
default-src provides a fallback source
policy when a more specific directive is not

4
00:00:18.047 --> 00:00:18.970
present.

5
00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:25.742
script-src controls script sources and
script execution patterns.

6
00:00:25.822 --> 00:00:31.937
style-src controls style sheets and
style-related behavior.

7
00:00:32.017 --> 00:00:35.247
img-src controls image sources.

8
00:00:35.327 --> 00:00:41.558
connect-src controls destinations for
fetch, XHR, WebSocket,

9
00:00:41.638 --> 00:00:44.176
and similar connections.

10
00:00:44.256 --> 00:00:47.487
font-src controls font loading.

11
00:00:47.567 --> 00:00:53.105
Other directives address important browser
behaviors.

12
00:00:53.185 --> 00:00:58.262
frame-src controls where frames can be
loaded from.

13
00:00:58.342 --> 00:01:06.764
object-src controls plugin-like object
resources and is often locked down tightly in

14
00:01:06.844 --> 00:01:08.575
modern policies.

15
00:01:08.655 --> 00:01:13.616
base-uri restricts where a base element
can point,

16
00:01:13.696 --> 00:01:19.350
which helps prevent surprising URL
resolution behavior.

17
00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:23.699
form-action limits where forms can submit.

18
00:01:23.779 --> 00:01:31.855
These directives reduce the risk that
injected markup or unexpected page behavior

19
00:01:31.935 --> 00:01:40.127
can redirect data or load active content
from places the application did not intend.

20
00:01:40.207 --> 00:01:46.668
Directive meaning depends on resource type
and browser behavior.

21
00:01:46.748 --> 00:01:54.479
A source expression that is appropriate
for images may be too broad for scripts.

22
00:01:54.559 --> 00:02:02.635
A policy that seems strict may still allow
behavior through a fallback directive if

23
00:02:02.715 --> 00:02:05.946
a specific directive is missing.

24
00:02:06.026 --> 00:02:11.564
Teams should read policies as
resource-specific rules,

25
00:02:11.644 --> 00:02:15.221
not as a single universal allowlist.

26
00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:21.762
The safest policies are explicit about
high-risk resource types,

27
00:02:21.842 --> 00:02:27.380
especially scripts, frames, forms, and
object content.
